    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d895">

                <pb facs="1320" n="1306" sameAs="frus:frus1948v06/pg1306"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Korea) /9–2048: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Special Representative in Korea</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Muccio</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-09-20">September 20,
                        1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>64. Cite Zpol 10993. Remytel 62, September 20.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d895-f1"/>
                    Following is text of statement issued to US press correspondents by Rhee
                    today.</p>
                <pb facs="1321" n="1307" xml:id="pg1307"/>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“If the report that the Soviet forces have decided to withdraw from North
                        Korea by the end of this year is true, I believe they are trying to do the
                        right thing, not only for the interests of Korea and other powers involved,
                        but they should have done so sooner for their own interests as well. I know
                        that US Army will not stay one day more than is necessary. The Soviet forces
                        remain in order to make the Communist regime safe and secure in North Korea,
                        I believe they have achieved their objective. However, they should not have
                        entertained any fear on that account, for no armed force in the South,
                        either Korean or foreign, ever intended to disturb the peace of the North.
                        It is our intention, as it has been always, to solve the problem peacefully
                        by ourselves, it is highly advisable for the Soviets to keep their hands off
                        and allow the Korean people to settle this question free from any foreign
                        interference.</p>
                    <p>I hope that the US will not play into the Soviet game by making any agreement
                        without consulting us.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d895-f2"/> Although Korea and
                        the US have no written agreement for mutual defense, yet we have been
                        working in unison in mutual interest of security of both nations and also
                        for the maintenance of democratic institutions. The Koreans have been
                        struggling under heavy odds to remain loyal to this principle, and I hope
                        that US will do the same.[”]</p>
                </quote>
                <p>Department pass to Moscow.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d895-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Muccio</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d895-f1" n="1" xml:id="d895-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d895-f2" n="2" xml:id="d895-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 75, September 23, from Seoul, Ambassador Muccio commented on this
                    sentence: “The wording of Rhee’s statement may have been motivated by local
                    widespread rumors current that US and Soviet Union have reached secret agreement
                    re Korea.” (740.00119 Control (Korea)/9–2348)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d895-f3" n="3" xml:id="d895-f3-content">
                <p>This was done the same day.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
